window.VD=window.VD||{};window.VD["skoda/fabia-4-pj"]={"fabia-4-pj-activate-daytime-running-lights-drl-only-in-auto-position":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Enables daytime running lights (DRL) only when the headlight control switch is in the “auto” position.  <br/><br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“31347</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – At the very top of this page, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>From this drop-down menu, select “<strong>Daytime running lights—Daytime running lights only in the AUTO position</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>On this menu, select<strong>“active</strong>” and confirm.<br/></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/5Q0-937-084-Q_Module09_Adaptation_drl_auto_switch-c7669352.webp\"/></figure>","fabia-4-pj-activate-lane-assist-memory-function":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to store the Lane Assist function in memory. If you deactivate it, the Lane Assist will not automatically resume the next time you start up.  <br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1 </strong>– Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostic socket or OBDeleven box.<br/><br/><strong>2 </strong>– Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program or the OBDeleven application.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 (For Camera A5 5Q0 / 3Q0) :</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>” then click on “.<strong>A5 – Frt Sens. Drv.  Assist</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“20103</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – On this page, at the very top, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>In this drop-down menu, choose “<strong>Personalization of lane dept. warning Cl.  15 on</strong>“<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu, but at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>On this menu, select<strong>“last setting </strong>” and confirm.</p>\n<hr/>\n<p><strong>Line 1 (For Camera A5 2Q0):</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>” then click on “.<strong>A5 – Frt Sens. Drv.  Assist</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>Coding – 07</strong>” then click on<strong>“Long Coding Helper</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Select byte<strong>“09</strong>”<br/>Then check Bit<strong> 0-1</strong><strong>“03 Configuration_for_lane_departure_warning_Kl15, Last_setting</strong>”.</p>","fabia-4-pj-activating-the-progressive-interior-lighting":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> turns interior lights (door knobs, dashboard, center console) on and off gradually. By default, interior lights are switched on and off immediately with the headlights. Activating this function will result in a slow fade effect each time the interior lights are switched on or off. (Interior Light Fade in/out)     <br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“31347</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – At the very top of this page, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>From this drop-down menu, select “<strong>Interior lighting-KL58 Einschalten mit Rampe</strong>“<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>On this menu, select<strong>“active</strong>” and confirm.</p>\n<figure>\n</figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/kl58rampe-e361a4ce.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>OBDeleven:</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/KL58rampeOBDeleven-1fdea7c1.webp\"/></figure>","fabia-4-pj-automatic-door-unlocking-with-dsg-in-p-position":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Automatically unlocks all doors when the DSG automatic transmission is shifted to the “P” position.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/><strong>1 –</strong> Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2 –</strong> Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>3 –</strong> Click on <strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on <strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.”</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>4 –</strong> Click <strong>“Security Access – 16,”</strong> then enter the code <strong>“31347”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>5 –</strong> Click on <strong>“Adaptation – 10”</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>6 –</strong> At the very top of this page, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>From this drop-down menu, select <strong>“Access control – Autounlock NAR”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>7 –</strong> On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu toward the bottom<br/>.<br/>From this menu, select <strong>“active</strong>, <strong>“</strong> then confirm.</p>","fabia-4-pj-automatic-window-closure-when-locked":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> automatically closes the windows when the car is locked. A new menu will be activated in door settings to deactivate the option if required.   <br/><br/><br/><strong>Warning! There are 2 lines of coding to do here. </strong> <br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“31347</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – On this page, at the very top, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>In this drop-down menu, choose “ZV Komfort Fensterheber – Komfortschliessen Brasilien”.<strong><strong>ZV Komfort Fensterheber – Komfortschliessen Brasilien</strong></strong>“<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>On this menu, select<strong>“active</strong>” and confirm.<br/></p>\n<p><strong>Line 2:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“31347</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>6</strong> – On this page, at the very top, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>In this drop-down menu, choose<strong>“ZV Komfort Fensterheber – <strong>BAP Komfortschliessen Brasilien</strong></strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>On this menu, select<strong>“active</strong>” and confirm.</p>","fabia-4-pj-comfort-turn-signal":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven Skoda Fabia 4 PJ\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Fabia4-j-1024x683-347d3c73.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to set the number of flashes for the “Comfort flashing” option.  <br/><br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“31347</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – On this page, at the very top, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>In this drop-down menu, choose<strong>“Turn signal control-Komfortblinken Blinkzyklen</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu at the bottom of the page.<br/>On this menu, choose between<strong>“3</strong>” and<strong>“5</strong>” and confirm.<br/></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/5Q0-937-084-Q_Module09_Adaptation_clignotant_confo-f1a2f919.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><em>Information: If, after coding, the number of flashes has not changed, on your MIB screen Menu -&gt; Settings -&gt; Factory settings -&gt; Lighting (Your interior lighting configurations will also be reset).</em></p>","fabia-4-pj-daytime-running-light-deactivation-drl-using-the-handbrake":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven Skoda Fabia 4 PJ\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Fabia4-j-1024x683-347d3c73.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to deactivate the daytime running lights (DRL) using the handbrake.  <br/><br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“31347</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – On this page, at the very top, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>In this drop-down menu, choose<strong>“Daytime running lights-Tagfahrlicht Dauerfahrlicht bei Handbremse abschalten</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>On this menu, select<strong>“active</strong>” and confirm.<br/></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Golf-7-5G---Desactiver-les-feux-de-jours-DRL-avec--6d661897.webp\"/></figure>","fabia-4-pj-daytime-running-lights-drl-on-off-via-display":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven Skoda Fabia 4 PJ\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Fabia4-j-1024x683-347d3c73.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Allows you to turn the daytime running lights (DRL) on or off from the CAR Menu.  </p>\n<p>BE AWARE of your country’s regulations and vehicle inspection requirements regarding daytime running lights!  <br/>However, if you’re coding in your car to avoid using too much of your battery, this is a good solution…  </p>\n<p><br/>Procedure: <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket or OBDeleven.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/>VCDS : </p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“31347</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – At the very top of this page, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>In this drop-down menu, select “<strong>Daytime running lights—Tagfahrlicht—Activation possible via BAP or control sequence</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>On this menu, select<strong>“active</strong>” and confirm.</p>\n<figure>\n</figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/5Q0-937-084-Q_Module09_Adaptation_feux_jour_drl-0a93900d.webp\"/></figure>\n<hr/>\n<p><strong>OBDeleven:</strong></p>\n<figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/obdeleven09-d37c6d29.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/obdelevenadapt-0d774aea.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/obdelevenaussenlicht-0747a95f.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/obdeleventagfahrlicht-78f4d30c.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/obdeleventagfahrlichtactive-e1f21fc0.webp\"/></figure>\n</figure>","fabia-4-pj-deactivate-open-door-alarm-with-ignition":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven Skoda Fabia 4 PJ\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Fabia4-j-1024x683-347d3c73.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to deactivate the alarm which is triggered when the door is opened and the contact is in position P.  <br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“17 – Instruments</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – On this page, at the very top, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>In this drop-down menu, choose<strong>“Ignition active message</strong>” / ”  <strong>Message ignition active. trigger </strong>  “<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – On this same page, you’ll find a second drop-down menu at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>On this menu, select<strong>“tbd</strong>” /<strong>“No display</strong>” / “<strong>Aucun affichage</strong>” then confirm.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/5Q0-937-084-Q_Module09_Adaptation_ignition_active_-e1e03128.webp\"/></figure>","fabia-4-pj-deactivate-switch-on-low-beam-message":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Disables the warning message or dialog prompting the driver to turn on the low beams when the headlight switch is in position 0.  </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/allumerfeuxdecroisement1-1024x609-756a4f05.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“31347</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – At the very top of this page, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>In this drop-down menu, select “<strong>Außenlicht_uebergreifend-Fahrlichtwarnung_Hinweis_Konfig</strong>” / ” <strong>driving light_hint_config</strong> ”  <br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu toward the bottom of the page.<br/>From this menu, select “<strong>kein_Hinweis</strong>” and then confirm.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/allumerfeuxdecroisement2-f3950d1b.webp\"/></figure>","fabia-4-pj-delete-belt-alarm":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven Skoda Fabia 4 PJ\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Fabia4-j-1024x683-347d3c73.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To suppress the audible alarm and the warning light on the speedometer when driving without a seatbelt.  <br/><br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“17 – Instruments</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>” then<strong>“Long Coding Helper</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select byte <strong>0</strong>:<br/>Bit <strong>2-4</strong>: select <strong>“Seat Belt Warning inactive”</strong> from the drop-down list.</p>\n<p><strong><em>OR</em></strong></p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“20103</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – On this page, at the very top, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>In this drop-down menu, choose <strong>“Deactivate</strong> <strong>Seat belt warning”</strong> / <strong>“</strong> <strong>Désactiver l’avertissement de ceinture”.</strong> <br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu, at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>On this menu, select<strong>“Oui</strong>” /<strong>“Yes”</strong> and confirm.</p>\n<figure>\n</figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/8U0_920_940_Module17_Adaptation_desactivate_seat_b-77d3b532.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/voyant_ceinture_de_securite-296648a9.webp\"/></figure>","fabia-4-pj-door-lock-unlock-confirmation-beep-horn":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to set confirmation of door locking/unlocking with beep (alarm) or horn.  <br/><br/><br/><strong>Warning!! There are 5 lines of code to write here—actually, 6. </strong> <em>(After programming, if it doesn’t work, enable the option on your screen in CAR – Open/Close Settings – check “Audible Confirmation”)</em> <br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“31347</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – At the very top of this page, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>From this drop-down menu, select “<strong>Acknowledgement signals—Unlock acoustic feedback</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu located toward the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>From this menu, select “<strong>Yes</strong>” and then confirm.<br/><br/><strong>Line 2:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“31347</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – At the very top of this page, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>In this drop-down menu, select “<strong>Acknowledgement signals—Lock Akustische Rückmeldung</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu located toward the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>From this menu, select “<strong>Yes</strong>” and then confirm.<br/><br/><strong>Line 3:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“31347</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – At the very top of this page, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>From this drop-down menu, select “<strong>Acknowledgement signals-Akustische Rueckmeldung global</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>On this menu, select<strong>“active</strong>” and confirm.<br/><br/><strong>Line 4:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“31347</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – At the very top of this page, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>From this drop-down menu, select “<strong>Acknowledgement signals—Menuesteuerung Akustische Rückmeldung</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>On this menu, select<strong>“active</strong>” and confirm.<br/><br/><strong>Line 5:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“31347</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – At the very top of this page, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>From this drop-down menu, select “<strong>Acknowledgement signals—Duration of the acoustic feedback from the single horn</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu, but at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>On this, choose<strong>“normal</strong>” or<strong>“kurz</strong>” (for a short signal), then confirm.<br/><br/><strong><br/>!!! If your vehicle is not equipped with an alarm, here is the code you need to change to activate the horn </strong>  :<br/><br/><br/><strong>Line 6:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“31347</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – At the very top of this page, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>From this drop-down menu, select “<strong>Acknowledgement signals—Akustische Rückmeldung Signalhorn</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>On this menu, select<strong>“active</strong>” and confirm.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/IMG_7559-1024x495-1f01c127.webp\"/></figure>","fabia-4-pj-fan-speed-display-on-air-conditioning-in-auto-mode":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to display fan speed when automatic air-conditioning mode is activated. (Climatronic fan speed display Auto mode)  <br/><br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>” then click on<strong>“08 – Climatronic</strong>” /<strong>“08 – Auto HVAC</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>” then click on<strong>“Long Coding Helper</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Select byte<strong>“11</strong>”<br/>Then select Bit<strong>“0-1</strong>”:<strong>“Blower display in Auto mode, enabled</strong>”.<br/></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/bitaffichageclimauto-48c9b868.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/affichageclimautomqba1-scaled-434fefd1.webp\"/></figure>","fabia-4-pj-increase-gas-pedal-sensitivity":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven Skoda Fabia 4 PJ\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Fabia4-j-1024x683-347d3c73.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to increase accelerator pedal sensitivity. (better acceleration response with DSG, more significant than with manual).  <br/><br/>Proceed : <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/></p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong> <br/><br/> 3 – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“44 – Steering Assistance</strong>“.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“19249</strong>“.  </p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – On this page, at the very top, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>In this drop-down menu, choose<strong>“Commutation profil de conduite</strong>” /<strong>“Driving profile switchover</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu, but at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>On this, choose the value: “<strong><em>directly: controlled by threshold value</em></strong>” / “<em><strong>Direct controlled over threshold value</strong></em>” then confirm.</p>\n<figure>\n</figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/IMG_7198-330b03e4.webp\"/></figure>","fabia-4-pj-key-operation-with-ignition-on":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Allows you to use the key and lock the vehicle with the ignition or engine running. (Very practical with a Keyless Kessy key)  <br/><br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“31347</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – On this page, at the very top, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>In this drop-down menu, choose<strong>“Access control-Funk bei Klemme 15 ein</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>On this menu, select<strong>“active</strong>” and confirm.</p>","fabia-4-pj-lap-timer":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to add the stopwatch to the ODB/Virtual Cockpit.  <br/><br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“17 – Instruments</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>” then click on<strong>“Long Coding Helper</strong>” .<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Select byte<strong>“1</strong>”<br/>Then check Bit<strong>“3</strong>”:<strong>“Lap Timer active</strong>”.</p>\n<figure>\n</figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/GOLF-7-VAGCOM---CHECK-DES-AIGUILLES-1-dab4b41b.webp\"/></figure>","fabia-4-pj-light-assist-always-active":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven Skoda Fabia 4 PJ\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Fabia4-j-1024x683-347d3c73.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> automatically activates the high beam assistant on start-up if it was active before the ignition was switched off.  <br/><br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“31347</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – On this page, at the very top, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>In this drop-down menu, choose<strong>“Assistance light functions-Fernlichtassistent Reset</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On the same page, there’s a second drop-down menu at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>Select<strong>“not active</strong>” and confirm.<br/></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/fernlicht-6b04f01d.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/lightassist-1a9a5499.webp\"/></figure>","fabia-4-pj-needle-check":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven Skoda Fabia 4 PJ\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Fabia4-j-1024x683-347d3c73.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to activate the scanning of the speedometer needles when the car is started. (Works with Virtual Cockpit) (Needle Sweep)  <br/><br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“17 – instruments</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>” then click on<strong>“Long Coding Helper</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Select byte<strong>“1</strong>”<br/>Then check Bit<strong>“0</strong>”:<strong>“Gauge Test/Needle Sweep (Stading) active</strong>”.<br/></p>\n<figure>\n</figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/GOLF-7-VAGCOM---CHECK-DES-AIGUILLES-1-dab4b41b.webp\"/></figure>","fabia-4-pj-rear-lights-activated-with-daytime-running-lights-drl":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Enables the rear lights to turn on when the daytime running lights (Scandinavian DRL) are activated (This method turns on the instrument panel lights) <br/><br/><br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“31347</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Click on “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – At the very top of this page, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>In this drop-down menu, select “<strong>Daytime running lights—Tagfahrlicht—Dauerfahrlicht activated in addition to parking lights</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu near the bottom of the page.<br/>From this menu, select “<strong>active</strong>” and then confirm.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/5Q0-937-084-Q_Module09_Adaptation_feux_arriere_drl-eff83f7a.webp\"/></figure>","fabia-4-pj-auxiliary-quantity-display-on-odb":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven Skoda Fabia 4 PJ\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Fabia4-j-1024x683-347d3c73.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Displays the amount of fuel needed to completely fill your tank on the ODB / Virtual Cockpit. <br/><br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“17 – Instruments</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>“, then on<strong>“Long Coding Helper</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Select byte “<strong>10</strong>“<br/>. Then check bit “<strong>4</strong>“: “Volume to Be Replenished”</p>\n<p>If it’s a hidden bit, you need to replace the <strong>0</strong> with a <strong>1</strong> in “<strong>Binary</strong>.”</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Golf-MKVII-Affichage-quantite-carburant-ODB-1024x3-6098b6ed.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/refuel-quantity-golf7-504de88f.webp\"/></figure>","fabia-4-pj-reprogramming-a-new-battery":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Allows you to reprogram or recode all data for the new battery after it has been replaced. <br/><br/><br/><strong>Watch out! There are 4 lines of coding to do here. </strong> <br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong><br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click “<strong>Select</strong>,” then click “<strong>19 – CAN Gateway</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click ” <strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – At the very top of this page, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>From this drop-down menu, select “<strong>Battery Adaptation—Nominal Battery Capacity</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu toward the bottom<br/>.<br/>From this menu, select the rated capacity listed on your battery. For example: “<strong>70Ah</strong>“<br/><br/><strong>Line 2:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click “<strong>Select</strong>,” then click “<strong>19 – CAN Gateway</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – At the very top of this page, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>From this drop-down menu, select “<strong>Battery Adaptation—Battery Technology</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu further down<br/>.<br/>From this menu, select the corresponding value<br/>. For example: For the VARTA E39, select “<strong>Etoffe</strong>” / “<strong>Fleece</strong>” for AGM.<br/><br/><strong>Line 3:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click “<strong>Select</strong>,” then click “<strong>19 – CAN Gateway</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – At the very top of this page, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>From this drop-down menu, select “<strong>Battery Adaptation—Battery Manufacturer</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu further down the page<br/>.<br/>From this menu, select the corresponding brand:<br/>For example: For the VARTA E39, select “<strong>VAO</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>Line 4:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click “<strong>Select</strong>,” then click “<strong>19 – CAN Gateway</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – At the very top of this page, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>From this drop-down menu, select “<strong>Battery Configuration—Battery Serial Number</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu toward the bottom<br/>.<br/>In this menu, enter the corresponding serial number:<br/>Note: Enter only the <strong>last 10 digits</strong> of the serial number.<br/></p>\n<hr/>\n<p><strong>Battery Status Detection Features:</strong></p>\n<p><br/>The battery status detection in the battery data module calculates the following values, which are transmitted via the LIN bus to the J533 diagnostic data bus interface:<br/>1 “battery not present” detection: if the battery data module does not detect a battery for<br/>more than 30 seconds, a corresponding bit is set to 1<br/>2 voltage that temporarily allows for optimal battery charging<br/>3 “engine start capability” bit. It indicates whether the battery is currently capable of starting<br/>the engine or not<br/>4. The amount of charge that can still be temporarily drawn from the battery before reaching the limit of<br/>its ability to start the engine<br/>5. The battery’s current state of charge<br/>6. Battery aging. Battery aging can be determined using parameters such a<br/>, including the battery’s energy storage capacity and instantaneous power output.<br/>7 battery open-circuit voltage<br/>8 battery internal resistance  </p>\n<p><strong>The battery sensor in the J367 battery monitoring control unit:</strong></p>\n<p>Knowing whether the battery has enough electrical energy to restart the engine is essential for the start-stop system to function properly.<br/>The control module is located directly on the “negative” terminal of the ground wire and is connected to the diagnostic interface via the LIN data bus.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/charge-batterie-c76863ff.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Signal Usage:</strong><br/>The battery sensor records the following values:<br/>– Battery temperature<br/>– Battery voltage<br/>– Charging current<br/>The battery temperature is calculated using a lookup table and the ambient temperature.<br/>It also allows for estimates of the battery’s charging time.<br/>Using the data obtained in this way, it is possible to adjust the charging control—particularly the charging voltage—to the battery’s state of charge and operating conditions. The goal is to increase the availability of the start-stop system by optimally utilizing data from the starter battery. </p>\n<p><strong>Consequences in the Event of a Malfunction</strong><br/>If the battery sensor is defective, the battery status can no longer be detected correctly.<br/>A fault is logged in the diagnostic interface’s fault memory. The start-stop system is deactivated. </p>\n<p><strong>Battery</strong></p>\n<p>The vehicle’s battery is housed in the engine compartment and protected by a battery tray. The size and type of battery depend on the engine version, equipment, and country-specific variant. Standard batteries, EFB batteries, and AGM batteries are used.<br/>Vehicles with gasoline engines and start/stop systems are equipped with an EFB battery, while vehicles with diesel engines and start/stop systems are equipped with an AGM battery. Vehicles with auxiliary heaters typically have an AGM battery.   </p>\n<p><strong>EFB</strong></p>\n<p>An EFB battery (short for Enhanced Flooded Battery) is a reinforced flooded battery. The positive plate inside the battery is coated with an additional layer of polyester mesh. This provides the battery’s active material with additional support on the plate. These batteries have greater resistance to charge-discharge cycles than standard batteries. During charging, an EFB battery is treated like a standard battery.    </p>\n<p><strong>AGM Battery</strong></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/batterie-agm-f4b81a74.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>In AGM (Absorbent Glass Mat) batteries, the battery electrolyte is retained within a microporous glass mat. An AGM battery is characterized not only by even greater resistance to charge-discharge cycles compared to an EFB battery, but also by its leak-proof design. When charging, follow the charger’s user manual and, if applicable, select the AGM battery program.  </p>\n<p>In the case of AGM batteries, the automotive battery electrolyte is retained within a microporous glass mat. In addition to<br/>increased resistance to charge-discharge cycles, an AGM battery is characterized by its high level of leak resistance. Sealing is of crucial<br/>importance, particularly when the battery is installed in<br/>the passenger compartment. When charging, follow the charger’s user manual and, if necessary, select the program for<br/>AGM batteries.   </p>\n<p>Below, we can see the brand, serial number, and manufacturer of the battery</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/batterie-124711ff.webp\"/></figure>"};
